R {11063} {0} {<228>} {Dark winding path} {<228>( *<088>} {  The trees grow thickly along this stretch of the winding path.  Ancient sentinels guarding the secrets of the deeper and darker places in the marsh. Thick bushes with thorns and bright flowers choke the sides of the path and the sweet smell of nectar permeates the air.  Life, it seems, is everywhere, the cycle shown in so many ways, rotting logs, fungi feeding on dead things back in the dark.  The death screams of small rodents to some predator.  All these things you can hear and sometimes even see while traveling through the marshland.  The sounds, sights and smells are all of life, death and rebirth.  You can hear more clearly, as well, a source of water, larger than the small stagnant pools which form in the underbrush from the constant dripping of water from the trees and vines.  An errant breeze lightly brushes your cheek, and it brings with it the chanting you heard when first entering the marshlands, and you again wonder what the source of that sound could be as it tugs at something deep inside you; something primal and almost forgotten, yet still very real and very much a part of your being.   } {agroth} {} {forest} {} {1.000} {}
E {11064} {e} {e} {2} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
E {11062} {s} {s} {4} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {11064} {0} {<228>} {An overgrown path} {<228>( *<088>} {  Here the path becomes nearly impossible to follow as creepers and vines crawl over the muddy space between ancient trees.  Shafts of sunlight slant down through branches, which do not conceal as much as they seem to the west, though mushrooms and other fungi still flourish in the thick undergrowth.  Overhead, you hear a shrill scream as a bird of prey makes its hunting call.  All around, the sounds of life seem to stop, and a soft breeze gusts through the thick branches above you.  As the echoes of the cry dissipate, the sounds of life slowly return to your surroundings.  The smells of water and mud grow stronger to the north, and the path here is swampier than to the west, and there is the sound of a large splash from the north.   } {agroth} {} {forest} {} {1.000} {}
E {11049} {n} {n} {1} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
E {11063} {w} {w} {8} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {11065} {0} {<228>} {Steamy path} {<228>( *<088>} {  After passing the great tree you come to a slight clearing in the marshland.  Here the path is broad and the trees and undergrowth do not intrude.  The mud steams and fills the air with misty clouds of vapor as the mud bubbles, never seeming to dry at all.  The scent of stagnant water is heavy and the oozing brown muck clings to everything it touches.  In the distance, to the northwest, you can see the largest tree you have ever seen. From here it is difficult to make out much detail, though its branches seem to hang down like those of a weeping willow.  Also, you can finally determine where the primal chanting is coming from.  It, too, is coming from the northwest, and it would seem as if the tree shelters whatever, or whoever is chanting.  A King Fisher glides overhead and a small squeaking shriek comes from the undergrowth to the east, and you quickly glance around for other paths.  It would appear that after crossing the lake you can only go toward the large tree in the distance.   } {agroth} {} {forest} {} {1.000} {}
