R {12920} {0} {<228>} {The high ground} {<228>, `<088>} {  The Eldar, who remained neutral through the great war, and kept a historical account, instead, tell of how the battle fought here finally began to turn in favor of the forces of good.  Though there is little evidence now of the skirmishes, the Goblins from the north and the Dwarves from Juargan had finally reached the battlefield, both armies somewhat depleted, but still sizeable forces.  It was at this spot that the Dwarves under their king, who always takes the name of their homeland, began to turn the tide.  The Dwarven army formed into several wedges and with swinging hammers and axes, drove deep into the enemy lines.  Many summoned corpses fell to the dwarven high priests, and upon seeing the Goblin horde joining the battle, the cousins of the Juargan, the High Dwarves began to fight with renewed vigor.  The ground rises slightly at this point, and it is clear that this rise, unlike so many others, is not that of a burial mound, but a natural feature of the landscape.  This is where the forces of good were finally able to hold ground, at least briefly, for the battle was not won on this spot.  Many Elven archers, usually distrustful of the temperamental Dwarves, now fought alongside them, bow and axe alike decimating many warbands within the dark forces.  The evidence of the battle still lies all around.  Half buried in the new earth, a brightly shining axe of dwarven steel seems to have never aged.  A few small arrowheads, both of Goblin and Elven make litter the bare earth where treasure hunters have been digging.  There doesn't seem to be much, if any, magical damage to this part of the ground, however to the south is a different story.  The sages say that is where the magical battle, too began to change.   } {mesolar} {} {field} {} {1.000} {}
