R {23596} {0} {<248>} {A Good View Isn't Necessarily A Good Thing} {<248>[ ]<088>} {  You have an excellent view of Ring Number One from this section of the  stands. Unfortunately the act in Ring Number One consists of a timid looking  liger tamer attempting to fend off the hungry advances of a large number of  ligers. The ligers themselves don't look as majestic as they look toothy. And the more you look at the cage enclosing them all, the more you realize it is made of very flimsy wire. Not the sort of wire likely to hold back hungry  ligers when they smell tasty snacks sitting in the stands. Also the liger  tamer looks pretty puny, hardly more than a few good mouthfuls. All in all,  you think you'd better look for a new seat or better yet, a way out of the  circus. You head to the back of the tent, thinking you can cut your way out  easily enough. But although the material looks and feels like cheap cotton,  try as you might you cannot slice, rip or otherwise harm the material and  make your escape. } {nynewoods} {} {chessblack} {} {1.000} {}
E {23597} {e} {e} {2} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
E {23595} {s} {s} {4} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {23597} {0} {<248>} {Ring Number One, Now Serving Dinner} {<248>[ ]<088>} {  The Ring in this part of the Big Top is surrounded by a hastily constructed  wire cage. The cage itself is nowhere near high enough or strong enough to  keep the ligers inside, even if they were happy and content ligers, which  they are not. Instead the ligers pace around the ring, making little feints  at the hapless liger tamer trying to ward them off. It is quite clear that  they are simply playing with their food, much like a house cat plays with a  mouse. When they decide it is time, the tamer's chair and whip will be little protection against their claws and teeth. The liger tamer circles the ring,  trying very hard not to leave his back exposed. Why he doesn't just drop  everything and run is incomprehensible, though perhaps he knows the ligers  will stop him before he gets far enough. From up above a trapeze artist  swoops into the cage, mere inches off the ground. One of the ligers takes a  swipe at him, managing to tear off a goodly part of his costume before his  momentum takes him into the air once more. } {nynewoods} {} {chessblack} {} {1.000} {}
