R {23668} {0} {<228>} {Could Things Get Any Worse?} {<228>( *<088>} {  The path through this part of the woods is overgrown and muddy as though  perhaps an underground stream runs through here. Depressions in the path have filled with brackish water. The mud sucking at your feet and the need to swat away swarms of buzzing mosquitoes have slowed your progress considerably. A  large, flat rock over by the cliff attracts your attention and you go over to scrape the mud off your feet and rest. Little light penetrates the woods here and the ground is heavily covered with rotting leaves and branches which give off a sharp odor of decay. A pile of rocks covered in moss and vines rests  against the cliff wall. There is a small black hole at the top of this, as  though it was once a cave opening, but it is too small and dark and the rocks too heavy to be worth exploring. As you stand something crunches under your  feet. Kicking away the leaves you see it is a bone. Curious, you kick away  more of the leaves in the area and come to realize that this is the final  resting place for a number of creatures of various sizes, some humanoid. This no longer seems like a good place to rest and you determine to leave. } {nynewoods} {} {forest} {} {1.000} {}
E {23569} {n} {n} {1} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
E {23669} {s} {s} {4} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
E {23667} {w} {w} {8} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {23669} {0} {<228>} {The Path I Walk} {<228>( *<088>} {  The path you are following now can scarcely be called that, as it has  degenerated into a muddy overgrown trail. A creek runs along the eastern side of the path, its edges choked with water plants. Vines hang from the trees  making forward progress difficult. Flowering plants that you have not seen  elsewhere crowd around the trunks of the trees, giving off a sickly aroma. At long last you break through to the end of the trail and are faced with a  cliff wall, covered in moss and vines. The trail curves to the west, seeming  to get wider and drier. You can barely make out the trail to the north, its  route hidden by vines and bushes. The creek makes a sharp turn to the east,  flowing under a heavy, inpenetrable curtain of vines. In the setting sun its  waters glow a blood red and you hope this crimson creek is not a portent of  the future. If you want to see what lies to the east, it seems the only way  is under the vines, which means jumping into the creek. } {nynewoods} {} {forest} {} {1.000} {}
