R {23671} {0} {<228>} {Southern Hospitality} {<228>( *<088>} {  As soon as you enter this part of the woods you notice signs of occupation. Cigarette butts litter the path and empty bottles have been thrown into the  bushes, which have been picked clean of berries. Here and there you see  evidence of a small fire and the bones of a woodland animal. A strong smell  of decaying flesh fills your nostrils, overpowering the woodsy smell and a  cloud of flies rises from something at the side of the path, disturbed by  your approach. Your stomach churns as you gaze upon the headless,  maggot-ridden corpse of a hapless adventurer, the body stripped of all  valuables and tossed carelessly by the side of the path to rot. Nevertheless, it is now a matter of honor for you to continue and soon you  emerge into a clearing, the central feature being a stone fire pit which is  lit despite the heat of the day. A sagging tent, really no more than faded  pieces of cloth held up by branches is against the rock wall. Sharpened  wooden poles have been pounded into the ground in front of the tent. These  are topped with skulls, except for one, which bears the rotting head of the  corpse you'd found, the bone just beginning to be visible beneath the putrid  flesh. Now you know the fiendish purpose behind cutting off the head and  taking it away. Wooden targets have been nailed to several trees and an axe  is embedded in the bull's eye of one of them. Gripping your own weapon more  tightly, you step out into the middle of the clearing. } {nynewoods} {} {forest} {} {1.000} {}
E {23670} {e} {e} {2} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
E {23666} {n} {n} {1} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
E {23672} {w} {w} {8} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {23672} {0} {<228>} {The Deep South} {<228>( *<088>} {  You have now entered the southernmost part of the woods. Through the trees  you can see the high cliff faces which hem the area in, containing it. Here  and there along the path you can pick out the tracks of some woodland animal  which passed here before you. In places the path is eroded, creating  treacherous potholes and exposing tree roots. You must pay close attention to where you walk, watching the path ahead of you. You hear a snort and a grunt  and look up to see a wild pig. It appraises you for a minute with its small,  mean eyes before squealing and blundering off through some rye which is  growing wild by the side of the path. Long after it is out of sight you can  hear its passage through the woods. The pig's flight trampled some of the  undergrowth and a flash of white catches your eye. When you go over to  investigate you discover a skeleton, the skull grinning at you, the clothes  mostly rotted away. There can be no doubt as to how the unfortunate victim  met his end. An arrow protrudes from one eye socket and a hatchet blade is  still firmly wedged into a vertebra in the neck. You wonder what could have  sparked such animosity. Was it a settling of accounts between rival factions? Perhaps the work of a psychopathic serial killer? Looking around you see that a number of trees have arrows embedded in them, as though they'd been used  for target practice. From the east the sound of voices drifts in on the wind. With extra vigilance, you continue on your way. } {nynewoods} {} {forest} {pk} {1.000} {}
E {23671} {e} {e} {2} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
E {23665} {n} {n} {1} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {23673} {0} {<228>} {A Patch Of Higher Ground} {<228>; ;<088>} {  This area is drier, firmer ground although it's still very muggy. The creek  disappears here, its waters going through a small hole in the rocky cliff  which blocks passage to the north. Debris is piled up at the entrance, the  flotsam of ages. Flowers shaped like upside down bells grow in patches  alongside wide stemmed grasses. A number of cypresses grow here, their  branches capable of hiding any number of creatures. Reeds and other water  plants cluster around the edges of the water, some of them showing signs of  having been trampled down by large animals. There is even a large circular  depression as though it is a nest or sleeping area though thankfully it is  empty at the moment. A large tree lies broken, its trunk cracked and split  into jagged pieces, although there's no obvious cause of its destruction. Twigs snap and brush rustles as small animals move through the undergrowth. } {nynewoods} {} {swamp} {} {1.000} {}
E {23674} {e} {e} {2} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
E {23675} {s} {s} {4} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {23674} {0} {<228>} {Now You've Seen It All... Or Have You?} {<228>; ;<088>} {  You've reached drier land and can walk quite easily here. The trees are  firmly rooted in the soil, large rocks are scattered about and the water  plants give way to grasses, flowers and berry bushes. There are still pools  of water here and there but it is mostly clear and clean and the largest  thing in them are some small black fish. A frog croaks and another frog  answers before hopping into one of the pools. A brown speckled bird looks  keenly at you from a tree branch, gives out a sharp cry and takes flight. Several more birds sit in the branches of the bushes eating berries. You  press farther on walking into taller growth, almost like wheat, perhaps it is some grain growing wild. It reaches up past your waist and closes up behind  you, obliterating all signs of the path you took to reach here. Moving  farther into the field you see a strange and unexpected sight. A scarecrow  has been placed on a post high above this "wheat field" like a king surveying his domain. It doesn't seem to be doing much good as small mice scurry amid  the stalks picking up fallen grain and birds flit about the tops, scattering  grain as they feed. Bright white light momentarily illuminates the clearing,  perhaps a storm is on the way, though you don't hear thunder. It tricks your  eyes into thinking the scarecrow is moving. The light flashes again and this  time you realize it was no trick as he hitches himself down from the post and approaches. } {nynewoods} {} {swamp} {} {1.000} {}
