R {30803} {0} {<238>} {The Tomb of Tuthmoses II} {<138>~ ~<088>} {  What a mess!  Gold gleams from every corner of this modest crypt, but the funerary furniture, statues, and personal belongings of the late Tuthmoses II lay in a jumbled heap.  Even the walls are painfully bare, the heiroglyphs telling the story of his life covering barely a third of the available surface.  At first glance, one might wonder if graverobbers made it into the tomb, but this is not the case.  The sad fact is, the Pharaoh's sudden death at such an early age left no time to properly complete his final resting place.   } {dynasty} {} {desert} {} {1.000} {}
E {30802} {n} {n} {1} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {30804} {0} {<238>} {The Tomb of Tuthmoses I} {<138>~ ~<088>} {  Now /this/ is a tomb.  Tuthmoses I lived a full rich life, founding a dynasty of his own, and ruled Egypt wisely and well, and his crypt reflects that fact.  They almost ran out of room on the walls, so grand and glorious are the deeds spoken there in carved and painted heiroglyphs.  His treasures are neatly arranged, centered by the great stone sarcophagus that protects his remains.  The mummy is intact, ensuring that the Pharaoh has achieved paradise, laboring in the heavenly fields of Osiris.   } {dynasty} {} {desert} {} {1.000} {}
E {30802} {e} {e} {2} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {30805} {0} {<238>} {Deir el-Bahri} {<138>~ ~<088>} {  This round, broad valley is something special.  Backed by a high cliff that juts hundreds of feet into the air, it's the site for what will soon be one of the most magnificent architectural structures in all of Egypt.  Already the smooth slope of stone leading up to Hatshepsut's temple is in place, and each day a new column is added to the wide first level.  Hundreds of workers toil in the blazing sun to complete the work, and more than a few have died in the effort.  Surely, with so fantastic a monument built by Hatshepsut, her name will be remembered forever.   } {dynasty} {} {desert} {} {1.000} {}
