R {30816} {0} {<268>} {The Temple of Amon-ra} {<268>{<178>+<268>}<088>} {  The Plaza outside gives voice to the greatness of the Pharaoh, in and of herself a Living God.  The Temple of Amon-Ra honors the God she claims as her father, and naturally it's even more glorious.  Utterly breathtaking, its fifty-foot-high walls plated in solid gold, the Temple is a marvel to behold.  A glance in any direction reveals the riches of Egypt, and what mankind is willing to sacrifice for its chief deity.  The robes of the priests are sewn of the finest linens and furs; oil lamps of rare ivory are placed precisely to drench an enormous statue completely in light.   } {dynasty} {} {temple} {} {1.000} {}
E {30815} {s} {s} {4} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {30817} {0} {<138>} {The Palace of Hapuseneb} {<138>[ ]<088>} {  This is one of the less pretentious of the nobles' palaces.  Perhaps this is because Hapuseneb has never married, and therefore has no wife to spend lots of gold on spiffing up the place.  There are a few small decorative statues on pillars here and there, and only a few artistic drawings on the whitewashed walls.  The palace is, all in all, a rather dull residence.  Still, we are talking about a palace, and compared to the lodgings of an average citizen of Egypt, it's luxurious.   } {dynasty} {} {palace_room} {} {1.000} {}
