R {30839} {0} {<278>} {Offices of the Pharaoh} {<278>[ ]<088>} {  These offices are strictly a place of business, and therefore no extraneous expense was utilized in their building.  Stone blocks form strong walls upon which hang detailed maps of the Egyptian empire.  Some are togographical, some detailing the population and the goods they produce.  Still others keep the Pharaoh appraised of her military forces and their deployment.  Desks are covered with neat piles of papyrus, and dozens of scribes and bureaucrats wade steadily through the ever-growing sea of paper. In the center of it all is the Pharaoh herself, keeping a steady hand on the pulse of her nation from this, its heart.   } {dynasty} {} {inside} {} {1.000} {}
E {30831} {s} {open s;s} {4} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {30840} {0} {<278>} {The Pharaonic Chambers} {<278>[ ]<088>} {  Hatshepsut was never one to stint herself, and her chambers are lavish indeed.  The stone walls are plated with beaten gold, her own giant image etched deeply into the metal.  Lotus flowers overflow a dozen vases, filling the air with their sweet perfume.  Her dressing table is carved from pure ivory, a rare and precious mirror of burnished copper firmly fastened to it. Her couch, too, is ivory, enameled in shiny royal blue, and it's made more inviting by soft stuffed matching cushions.   } {dynasty} {} {inside} {} {1.000} {}
E {30832} {s} {open s;s} {4} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {30841} {0} {<278>} {Chambers of the Queen} {<278>[ ]<088>} {  The chambers reserved for the Pharaoh's Chief Wife are empty and dusty, bare even of furniture.  Hatshepsut resided here during her husband-brother's reign, but a decade has passed since his demise.  There's a niche in the wall where Aahmose, the Pharaoh's mother, once kept a statue of her patron deity.   } {dynasty} {} {inside} {} {1.000} {}
E {30833} {s} {open s;s} {4} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {30842} {0} {<278>} {Aset's Chambers} {<278>[ ]<088>} {  Aset was rather spoiled while her husband Tuthmoses II was still alive, and though Hatshepsut has cut back on the woman's allowance, these chambers are still the richest in the palace by far.  Gold gleams everywhere, covering every inch of the walls and ceilings, and the floors are piled several inches deep in expensive fur.  Loose jewels lay scattered about, large and small, and Aset's dressing table can barely be seen beneath dozens of pieces of jewelry.   } {dynasty} {} {inside} {} {1.000} {}
E {30834} {s} {open s;s} {4} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {30843} {0} {<278>} {Prince Tuthmoses' Chambers} {<278>[ ]<088>} {  These chambers are as functional as livable.  Although they are the residence of a member of the royal family, there are few frills here.  There are, however, many large tables, covered with battle plans.  Tuthmoses is a very competent warrior, and longs to prove his prowess (and his worthiness to be Pharaoh).   } {dynasty} {} {inside} {} {1.000} {}
E {30834} {w} {open w;w} {8} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {30844} {0} {<278>} {Princess Meryet's Chambers} {<278>[ ]<088>} {  Princess Meryet's quarters are a cheerful clutter, typical for the room of a teenage girl.  The emphasis is on a collection of clothes that bulge from chests, each piece more fashionable than the last.  Like her stepmother Aset, the princess has a greedy love for jewels, but at least the royal young woman has good taste in the gems she wears.  Nothing gaudy for her- simply perfection in everything she wears and collects.  There's an empty spot on her dressing table, which she reserves for the coveted silver Cobra Crown worn by the Queen of Egypt.  For now, it belongs to her sister, but who knows what the future may bring...  or how it may be manipulated?   } {dynasty} {} {inside} {} {1.000} {}
E {30835} {w} {open w;w} {8} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {30845} {0} {<278>} {Princess Neferura's Chambers} {<278>[ ]<088>} {  This set of chambers is calm and quiet, reflecting its resident's personality.  It's decorated in images of lotus flowers and soaring birds carved of pale rose quartz and set into the walls.  While there are plenty of furnishings, they are not overly fancy, despite a tendency toward silver and copper.  These rooms are neatly kept, every belonging of the Princess well-organized.   } {dynasty} {} {inside} {} {1.000} {}
E {30836} {w} {open w;w} {8} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {30846} {0} {<278>} {The Royal Nursery} {<278>[ ]<088>} {  A rather large chamber with plenty of running room for young children, the nursery is a bright and cheerful place.  Mats line the floor near the walls, and chests full of colorful toys are everywhere.  There is one clear spot where the younglings gather for lessons.  The walls are painted in bright primary colors, and the ceiling shows images of the god Amon-Ra in his golden sun barge sweeping across the clear blue sky.  Fully royal children do not reside here- this is where the illegitimate children of the royal concubines are raised.   } {dynasty} {} {inside} {} {1.000} {}
E {30836} {n} {open n;n} {1} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
