R {37948} {0} {<228>} {A rocky spot in the plains} {<228>, `<088>} {    The rockslide, to the south, blocks any travel in that direction and the  cliff that juts out from the mountains helps to discourage southerly travel.  To the west, the range of foothills continues. All around this area, you see  evidence of the violence of the rockslide. The ground is dotted with large  and small rocks. The grass is flattened in spots and many dips and holes show  where the larger stones bounced as they fell. To the west, the way only  becomes tougher as the foothills themselves seem to have suffered some damage  from the event. The damage does seem localized, however. To the east, the  ground is flat and open and, to the north, you notice the foothills begin to  take a slightly easterly direction. Far to the north, you can see the eaves  of a large forest. } {nulan} {} {field} {} {1.000} {}
E {37947} {e} {e} {2} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
E {37957} {n} {n} {1} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
E {37949} {w} {w} {8} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {37949} {0} {<228>} {On a treacherous slope in the foothills} {<228>. ^<088>} {  The ground here rises sharply toward the western mountain range. It is very  rocky and pitted with small craters and holes. There are a few broken  saplings and a couple of stunted trees growing here but there is not much of  interest around. To the south, a cliff face blots out the view while, back to the east, a jumbled area eventually gives way to the flat plains once more. To the north, the foothills begin to wind their way east, though still on a  northern track. } {nulan} {} {hills} {} {1.000} {}
E {37948} {e} {e} {2} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
E {37958} {n} {n} {1} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}

R {37954} {0} {<228>} {A trampled area} {<228>, `<088>} {    The land around here is trampled almost flat.  Leading in all directions, the tracks of many large hoofed animals are clear in the crushed vegetation.  A soft mound of dirt, in the center of the trampled area, evidences the now  destroyed, former home of a prairie dog.  The broken stems of grass, wild  flowers and clover stick up like flexible stakes, dried and fragile.  Small  shoots of new vegetation can be seen growing up through the trampled earth  and you wonder what chance they have of survival, since this seems to be a  well traveled area.   } {nulan} {} {field} {} {1.000} {}
E {36510} {e} {e} {2} {0} {} {1.000} {} {0.00}
